        "sourceKind": "standards-or-specification",
        "sourceCitation": "BIPM SI Brochure 9th ed. (2019): mass among the seven SI base quantities; kilogram defined by fixed h = 6.62607015e-34 kg m2 s-1 (2.3.1)."
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "stemma:phys.metre",
      "@type": [
        "skos:Concept",
        "qudt:Unit",
        "stemma:Unit"
      ],
      "name": "Metre",
      "type": "unit",
      "status": "canonical",
      "definition": "The metre (symbol: m) is the base unit of length in the International System of Units (SI). It is scientifically defined as the length of the path travelled by light in a vacuum during a time interval of 1/299,792,458 of a second. Exact: c=299,792,458 m/s. Agreed per BIPM SI Brochure 9th ed. 2019 redefinition, fixing c exact.",
      "sameDimensionalQuantities": [
        "distance",
        "width",
        "height",
        "displacement",
        "wavelength",
        "radius"
      ],
      "symbol": "m",
      "unit": "metre (m)",
      "governedBy": [
        "stemma:phys.dimensional-analysis",
        "stemma:phys.si-definitions"
      ],
      "externalIds": {
        "wikidata": "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11573"
      },
      "provenance": {
        "aiDrafted": false,
        "writer": "human:curator.001",
        "reviewer": "human:curator.001",
        "reviewedAt": "2026-09-23T04:35:19.817235+00:00",
        "sourceKind": "standards-or-specification",
        "sourceCitation": "BIPM SI Brochure 9th ed. (2019) §2.3.1, p130-132: The metre, symbol m, is the SI unit of length. It is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the speed of light in vacuum c to be 299792458 when expressed in m s-1. Exact: c=299,792,458 m/s. Agreed internationally."
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "stemma:phys.newtons-second-law",
      "@type": [
        "skos:Concept",
        "stemma:Law"
      ],
      "name": "Newton's Second Law",
      "type": "law",
      "status": "canonical",
      "definition": "Newton's second law of motion: the net external force on a system equals the time rate of change of its total linear momentum, ΣF = dp/dt; for constant mass this reduces to F = m a. Valid in classical (non-relativistic) mechanics in inertial reference frames. Stated by Isaac Newton as Lex II in Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica (1687); standard modern formulation per Halliday, Resnick, Walker 12th ed., Ch 5.",
      "symbol": "F_net = m a",
      "governedBy": [
        "stemma:phys.conservation-momentum",
        "stemma:phys.dimensional-analysis"
      ],
      "externalIds": {
